<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jagat Seth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jagat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>, meaning 'banker of the world', was the hereditary title of a Marwari banking family based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, granted by the Mughal emperor in 1723. The family managed the Bengal mint and revenue remittances and lent to nawabs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> alike. Its head Mahtab Chand joined the plot with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> that led to the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a> at Plassey in 1757.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jagat-seth.jpg" alt="Jagat Seth" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gangulybiswarup · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:House_Of_Jagat_Seth_Complex_-_Mahimapur_-_Murshidabad_2017-03-28_6165-6169.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The banking house was founded by Hiranand Shah, a Jain Oswal Bania from Nagaur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> in 1652. The family built up its business towards the last quarter of the 17th century, and by the 18th century it was perhaps the largest banking house in the country. In 1713 Manikchand helped Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a> financially in his bid to become Mughal emperor, a sign of the favour the family had gained at the Mughal court. The historian Ghulam Hussain Khan wrote that its wealth could not be described without seeming to exaggerate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Nawabs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshid-quli-khan">Murshid Quli Khan</a>, used the family's credit networks to send their annual tribute to the Mughal emperors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and the house held a monopoly of minting coins in Bengal. Alivardi Khan came to the throne of Bengal in a military coup that the Jagat Seths financed and planned. Fateh Chand suffered a great loss in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s sack of Delhi but kept his business going until his death on 2 December 1744. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> invasions of Bengal, Bargi mercenaries plundered the family mansion and carried off 25 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>.</p><p>In the 1750s the family's wealth was estimated at 140 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, the new nawab, demanded a tribute of 30 million rupees, Mahtab Chand refused and the nawab struck him. Mahtab Chand then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>'s conspiracy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, Krishnachandra Roy, Omichund, Yar Lutuf Khan, Rai Durlabh and others, and the bankers funded the British side. The conspirators were concerned with their own political futures and had no intention of founding British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a>, a later nawab, had several members of the family killed in 1763, among them Mahtab Chand and his cousin Swarup Chand, and their bodies were thrown from the ramparts of Munger Fort. Mahtab Chand's son Kushal Chand received the title at the age of 18, but once the British moved the treasury and mint to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a> a private banker at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> was far less needed, and the family declined. Its last member died in 1912, living on a British pension.</p><p>The family house in the Mahimapur quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a>, with a secret tunnel and an underground chamber, became a privately managed museum in 1980. It displays the family's coins, muslin and Banarasi saris embroidered with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> thread, and the house, its temple and the nearby ruins are State Protected Monuments.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jagat Seth family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The nawabs relied on Hindu bankers such as the <b>Jagat Seths</b> and on zamindars for revenue.</blockquote><a href="/state/nawabate-of-bengal">Nawabate of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
